# ZIMDNS example configuration.
# Strict line-oriented configuration loaded by the current parser.
#
# A `listen` line supplies the ADDRESS a mode binds. It does not select the
# mode: one CLI flag (--once-udp, --once-tcp, --serve-udp, --serve-tcp,
# --serve-dot, --serve-doh, --serve-xfr) still decides what runs, and each is
# matched to the `listen` entry with the same transport. So `--config
# examples/config.gdns` on its own starts nothing and exits 2 with
# "server run currently requires --once-udp, --once-tcp, --serve-udp,
# --serve-tcp, --serve-dot, --serve-doh, or --serve-xfr"; add a mode flag, for
# example `--serve-udp` to bind 0.0.0.0:1053 from the udp line below.

listen dns udp 0.0.0.0:1053
listen dns tcp 0.0.0.0:1053

blocklist file blocklists/ads.txt

zone home.arpa primary zones/home.arpa.zone

# Optional bounded forwarding policy. Both an ACL and a forwarder are required
# before recursion is enabled, on the UDP and the TCP ingress alike.
# recursion allow 127.0.0.0/8
# forwarder 127.0.0.1:5353
# The TLS form names the server certificate and SNI value; the port is the
# operator's, so a DoT forwarder names 853 explicitly.
# forwarder 9.9.9.9:853 tls dns.quad9.net
# The DoH form names the certificate and SNI value and its POST target.
# forwarder 1.1.1.1:443 doh cloudflare-dns.com /dns-query
# cookies validate current-secret previous-secret
#
# The source ports the forwarder may not use for its outgoing queries.
# forwarder-deny-port 53
#
# The client subnet source prefix is trimmed to this many bits before the
# query goes upstream (RFC 7871 section 7.1.1).
# client-subnet-max-prefix 24
#
# RFC 6303/6761/7793/8375 locally served zones are answered from this host by
# default, ahead of the cache and the forwarder above. 'local-zones off' is the
# RFC 6303 section 3 mechanism to disable them; examples/local-zones.gdns is a
# worked configuration for them.
# local-zones on

# The IXFR delta log file: an old client is still answered incrementally. The
# file sits beside this configuration, so the name is a bare one.
# ixfr-journal-file ixfr.log

# The TSIG key file: a signed request is verified and its answer is signed
# back. The file is re-read on SIGHUP, so a key can be added, changed, or
# removed without a restart.
# tsig-key-file tsig.key

# The NAT64 prefix the forwarder synthesizes AAAA records into, and the IPv4
# range it serves. Without one, nothing is synthesized.
# nat64-prefix 2001:db8:64::/96
#
# An IPv6 range whose AAAA records are not real data, so a name that holds
# only such records is answered with a synthesized address.
# dns64-exclude 2001:db8:1::/48
